WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:06.974
This is the largest protest in Poland

00:00:06.974 --> 00:00:10.968
since the fall of communism
more than 30 years ago.

00:00:10.968 --> 00:00:14.824
Hundreds of thousands are protesting,
in cities across the country,

00:00:14.824 --> 00:00:20.656
because the party in power here is on the verge
of eliminating a woman’s right to an abortion.

00:00:29.828 --> 00:00:32.171
At the heart of these protests are young women:

00:00:32.171 --> 00:00:35.879
those who are the most
threatened by the new ruling.

00:00:35.879 --> 00:00:40.726
But there are many, many others too.

00:00:40.726 --> 00:00:43.618
And that’s because these protesters
aren’t just concerned

00:00:43.618 --> 00:00:46.883
about the future of abortion rights in Poland.

00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:52.188
They’re worried about the future
of the country itself.

00:00:53.765 --> 00:00:58.534
In the late 1980s, Poland got rid
of its decades-long communist regime.  

00:00:58.534 --> 00:01:03.162
Soon, it became a thriving democracy,
with a strong catholic identity.

00:01:03.162 --> 00:01:08.750
It also passed one of the strictest laws on abortion in all of Europe.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.954
Under the law, women were only allowed 
to have an abortion under three circumstances:

00:01:13.954 --> 00:01:17.214
if the pregnancy was a threat
to the health of the mother,

00:01:17.214 --> 00:01:20.463
if it was the result of incest or rape,

00:01:20.463 --> 00:01:24.588
or if the fetus had severe
and irreversible abnormalities.

00:01:24.588 --> 00:01:27.937
The law has remained this way
for nearly 30 years. 

00:01:27.937 --> 00:01:31.498
And today, it makes Poland an outlier in Europe,

00:01:31.498 --> 00:01:37.185
where virtually every other country allows 
abortions under a broad range of circumstances.

00:01:37.185 --> 00:01:42.492
But some in Poland thought
those strict laws still weren’t enough.

00:01:42.492 --> 00:01:48.383
In 2015, Poland elected the right-wing party
Law and Justice into power.

00:01:48.383 --> 00:01:50.632
Shortly after, the party leader said:

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:02.441
"Very close to the totalitarian way of thinking,

00:02:02.441 --> 00:02:08.837
as far as women's health, women's rights,
and women's personhood is concerned."

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:15.338
In 2016, the party backed legislation 
to eliminate all three legal paths to abortion. 

00:02:15.338 --> 00:02:18.916
The legislation proposed imprisoning
women seeking abortions,

00:02:18.916 --> 00:02:22.600
and doctors who performed them,
for up to five years.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.182
They would even investigate miscarriages.

00:02:25.182 --> 00:02:28.193
"This is something that is incredible, you know?

00:02:28.193 --> 00:02:32.024
It really turns us back to the Middle Ages.

00:02:32.024 --> 00:02:36.913
The limitation of human rights went far too far."

00:02:36.913 --> 00:02:38.957
Thousands of women took to the streets,

00:02:38.957 --> 00:02:43.333
pushing back against the law,
in what were called the Black Protests.

00:02:43.333 --> 00:02:47.045
In the face of this opposition,
the legislation was withdrawn.

00:02:47.045 --> 00:02:50.784
Poland would still have one of the strictest abortion laws in Europe, 

00:02:50.784 --> 00:02:53.185
but not a total ban.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:56.716
But instead of giving up on the abortion ban,

00:02:56.716 --> 00:03:01.634
Law &amp; Justice started dismantling
the country’s ability to oppose one.

00:03:01.634 --> 00:03:04.993
In Poland’s democracy,
some legislation passed by Parliament

00:03:04.993 --> 00:03:07.216
is sent to the Constitutional Tribunal,

00:03:07.216 --> 00:03:10.084
to ensure it’s consistent
with the country's constitution.

00:03:10.084 --> 00:03:15.284
This court has 15 judges, and it serves
as the main check on the ruling party.

00:03:15.284 --> 00:03:18.603
It’s supposed to function as an independent
branch of the government. 

00:03:18.603 --> 00:03:23.767
But upon taking power, Law &amp; Justice
started to erode that independence.

00:03:23.767 --> 00:03:26.436
They refused to swear in several judges

00:03:26.436 --> 00:03:28.483
who had been appointed
by the previous government,

00:03:28.483 --> 00:03:32.140
and replaced them with judges
who would be loyal to them,

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:34.437
including the Tribunal president,

00:03:34.437 --> 00:03:38.919
who has the ability to determine
which judges hear what cases.

00:03:38.919 --> 00:03:44.351
And of the judges they have appointed legally,
many are actually fellow politicians,

00:03:44.351 --> 00:03:47.450
further compromising
the independence of the court.

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:54.325
This has created a tribunal in which
14 of 15 judges are aligned with the ruling party. 

00:03:54.325 --> 00:03:58.057
And the legitimacy of several
of them is widely disputed,

00:03:58.057 --> 00:04:01.422
both in Poland and internationally.

00:04:01.422 --> 00:04:06.632
This version of the court has never
decided a case against the ruling party.

00:04:06.632 --> 00:04:13.593
"The fact that this Constitutional Tribunal
is acting on the behalf of the political party

00:04:13.593 --> 00:04:18.963
is just the opposite of what
the Constitutional Tribunal should do."

00:04:19.805 --> 00:04:23.951
It was under these circumstances
that Law &amp; Justice sought a new ruling

00:04:23.951 --> 00:04:27.373
from the Constitutional Tribunal
on the country’s abortion laws,

00:04:27.373 --> 00:04:31.081
that would eliminate one of the three bases
for legal abortion:

00:04:31.081 --> 00:04:34.010
Severe fetal abnormalities.

00:04:34.010 --> 00:04:37.923
And while it might seem like that
would only prevent some abortions,

00:04:37.923 --> 00:04:43.541
fetal abnormalities are the reason
for 98% of abortions in Poland.

00:04:43.541 --> 00:04:46.947
In other words, without
technically banning abortions,

00:04:46.947 --> 00:04:50.970
the change would make them
virtually impossible.

00:04:50.970 --> 00:04:54.260
Nearly 80% of Poles
disagreed with this change.

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:59.183
But on October 22nd, the Constitutional
Tribunal ruled in its favor:

00:04:59.183 --> 00:05:05.414
That the overwhelming majority of abortions
in Poland were unconstitutional.

00:05:06.178 --> 00:05:08.732
The ruling brought Poles back into the streets,

00:05:08.732 --> 00:05:11.696
and in much bigger numbers than in 2016.

00:05:11.696 --> 00:05:15.408
"These protests are really different from
what we've seen for the last four years,

00:05:15.408 --> 00:05:19.547
because there are thousands and thousands
of young people attending these protests,

00:05:19.547 --> 00:05:21.308
organizing these protests,

00:05:21.308 --> 00:05:27.000
and also, these are the most grassroots, 
independent protests that we've ever had."

00:05:51.068 --> 00:05:54.422
In the face of the protests,
the government has delayed taking the steps

00:05:54.422 --> 00:05:56.929
that would make the ruling official.

00:05:56.929 --> 00:06:01.730
As of this video, it’s unclear when,
or if, they will.

00:06:01.730 --> 00:06:06.007
But these protests are as much about
how this ruling came together,

00:06:06.007 --> 00:06:08.089
as they are about the ruling itself.

00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:11.093
MARTA LEMPART: "It started with abortion.
Now we fight for freedom, for everything."

00:06:11.093 --> 00:06:13.469
For the rule of law."

00:06:13.469 --> 00:06:16.261
Outside of Poland, the world has noticed. 

00:06:16.261 --> 00:06:21.197
International organizations have stopped considering it a full democracy.

00:06:21.197 --> 00:06:27.415
And the changes to its legal system have put
Poland’s European Union membership at risk.

00:06:31.757 --> 00:06:35.916
30 years ago, Poland enthusiastically
embraced democracy.

00:06:35.916 --> 00:06:42.141
Today, its ruling party is trying to rig the
country’s democratic institutions in its favor.

00:06:42.141 --> 00:06:45.650
And it's the people
who are fighting to protect them.

00:07:04.678 --> 00:07:10.331
"Despite the fact that this government
is responsible for destroying

00:07:10.331 --> 00:07:13.958
all the democratic tools
that we have in our Constitution,

00:07:13.958 --> 00:07:19.573
you still have the nation,
the citizens in this country,

00:07:19.573 --> 00:07:24.473
who carry on the democratic values."

